so I need to identify the most critical missing piece from the earliest incomplete release batch.\n\n**Release 00.0 Status**: The foundational packages are mostly done—format, sys, and testutils all have their implementation files and tests. The magefiles are scaffolded as part of the initial setup, not something I need to generate.\n\n**Releases 01.1 through 01.3**: cat, sponge, and du are all complete with both main.go and test files present.\n\n**Release 02.0 and Beyond**: These are blocked on specs—true, false, yes, basename, dirname, tee, head, and seq all need specification work before code can be written.\n\n**Release 03.0 (wc)**: This is interesting because main.go exists but the test file is missing from the source code listing. The codestatus showing \"not started\" seems inconsistent with the actual code presence, so there's a discrepancy between what the analysis reports and what's actually in the repository.\n\n**Release 04.0 and 04.1 (ls)**: Specs are complete but no code has been started yet.\n\nThe real blockers are the pending specs for the 02.0 and 02.1 releases, and I need to verify whether wc's test file actually exists or needs to be written. I'm realizing that cmd/version might actually need to be created as part of the magefiles setup, but I don't have its PRD in the context provided, and there are no specs for it either. Looking back at the release 00.0 breakdown, format, sys, and testutils already have code in place, while magefiles is scaffolded but excluded from generation—which means cmd/version is the actual blocker here.\n\nNow I'm re-examining the implementation_status definitions more carefully: \"spec complete\" means the PRD, use case, and code all exist at the v1 tag, while \"specs written\" means those documents exist but code generation hasn't run yet. The source_code section shows what was previously generated, but on the working branch there's no generated code—it lives at the generation-merged tags instead. So when source_code contains .go files for a package, that package should already be considered complete.\n\nLooking at what's actually there: pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, and pkg/format all have code; the cmd packages (cat, du, sponge, wc) have code too, though wc might be missing its test file. Release 02.0 has pending specs, Release 03.0 has the wc main but possibly no tests, and Release 04.0/04.1 (ls) has complete specs but no code yet. The real question is whether wc_test.go exists or if that's a gap I need to address.\n\nActually, checking the spec status—wc's spec is marked complete, so the PRD, use case, and test suite all exist. Scanning through the releases by number, 00.0 is mostly done, 01.1 through 01.3 appear complete with both main and test files, but 02.0 and 02.1 are blocked on pending specs. For 03.0, wc's main is there but the test file might be missing.\n\nLooking ahead, 04.0 and 04.1 have complete specs but no code yet. The earliest place I can actually generate code is either 03.0 if the wc tests are missing, or 04.0 for the ls core implementation. Though 02.0 comes earlier with trivial utilities, those specs are still pending, and I can't write code without a PRD—plus proposing spec work might be outside my scope as the measure agent. Looking at the incomplete tasks, cmd/version from release 00.0 is missing from the codebase, but I don't have the PRD details for it so I can't confidently propose that. The wc command has main.go but is missing its test file, which I can work with since I have the full context for that release. The spec is complete. Let me check what PRD requirements are covered...\n\ncmd/wc/main.go implements prd005-wc (R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.4). There's no wc_test.go in source_code, though it might exist but just wasn't included. Looking at other packages like cat and du, they both show test files alongside their main implementations, but wc only shows main.go. The release 03.0 roadmap marks wc as spec_complete but code not started, which seems inconsistent with the existing main.go from an earlier generation run—there's some reorganization happening between the old rel01.1 scheme and the current roadmap structure.\n\nGiven the constraint of picking just one task, wc tests for release 03.0 seem like the highest priority since the implementation already exists but tests are missing, whereas ls at 04.0 would require building both spec and code from scratch. (release 04.0) - specs complete, no code\n\nGiven that P5 says \"test suites before code\" and tests come before further implementation, and P1 says focus on earliest incomplete release, I should prioritize wc tests.\n\nBut wait - I need to check what PRD requirements are in prd005-wc. The main.go header says \"Implements prd005-wc (R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.4)\". The test suite spec should tell me what tests are needed.\n\nI don't have the test suite spec or the full PRD in the provided context. But I can derive test requirements from the existing patterns:\n- cat_test.go tests R1.1, R1.2, R5.2, R5.3\n- du_test.go tests R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.5\n- sponge_test.go tests R1.1, R2.1, R3.1, R4.1\n\nSo wc_test.go should test the core wc functionality against the reference binary. Looking at the wc main.go, it implements line counting, word counting, byte counting in default mode, stdin reading when no files are provided, right-justified output with filenames, totals for multiple files, proper column ordering, SIGPIPE handling, and error message formatting. Following the pattern from other tests, I need to set up TestMain to build the reference binary first.\n\nFor the differential test, I should focus on the four core requirements: default counting behavior, stdin mode, output formatting with filenames, and the total line for multiple files. That's exactly 4 PRD sub-requirements, which fits the constraint. I'll skip error handling for now since it's not one of the core four, and keep the test within the 150-250 line budget.\n\nThe test structure will follow the established pattern with TestMain building the binary and locating the reference gwc, helper functions for file writing and program name normalization, and using the testutils.RunDiffTests framework to compare outputs.\n\nWriting the task description... /testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests API)\n      - cmd/cat/cat_test.go (reference pattern for differential test structure)\n      - cmd/du/du_test.go (reference pattern for differential test structure)\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd005-wc.yaml (PRD for wc utility)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-03.0.yaml (test suite spec for wc)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/wc/wc_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Differential tests for wc against GNU gwc reference binary\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement differential tests verifying prd005-wc R1.1: default mode counts lines, words, and bytes for single and multiple files, matching GNU gwc output byte-for-byte\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement differential tests verifying prd005-wc R1.2: wc reads from stdin when no file arguments are given and when '-' appears as a file argument\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement differential tests verifying prd005-wc R1.3: output format is right-justified counts followed by filename, matching GNU gwc column alignment\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement differential tests verifying prd005-wc R1.4: when more than one file is given, wc prints a 'total' line after the per-file lines\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Follow the established TestMain pattern from cmd/cat/cat_test.go and cmd/du/du_test.go to set up the differential testing framework\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use testutils.RunDiffTests with DiffTest structs for all test cases, comparing stdout, stderr, and exit code byte-for-byte between the Go binary and gwc\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Include a normalizeProgramName function that replaces 'gwc: ' with 'wc: ' in stderr output, following the pattern established in cmd/cat/cat_test.go and cmd/du/du_test.go\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"Create test files with writeTestFile helper following the existing pattern, using t.TempDir() for test isolation\"\n      - id: D5\n        text: \"Mark all test functions with t.Parallel() to enable concurrent execution\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/wc/wc_test.go compiles without errors\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go test ./cmd/wc/ passes when gwc is available on PATH\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Tests cover single-file, multi-file, stdin-no-args, and stdin-dash-arg invocations\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"Tests verify the total line appears when multiple files are given\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"Tests verify individual flag selection (-l, -w, -c) produces output matching gwc\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"All tests use testutils.RunDiffTests for byte-for-byte comparison\"\n\nI'm noticing AC5 about individual flag selection wasn't explicitly in my requirements, but looking at the main.go flags and R1.1 which covers the default mode, the individual flags are really part of that same requirement since the default behavior depends on whether flags are set. Testing the flags should be covered under R1.1 rather than as a separate criterion.\n\nI've got exactly 4 sub-requirements (R1.1 through R1.4) which fits the constraint, and comparing to similar test files like cat_test.go and du_test.go, I'm aiming for around 150-200 lines for wc_test.go. Let me make sure the task title and other formatting are correct before finalizing.\n\nNow I need to verify the actual file paths exist—prd005-wc.yaml should be in docs/specs/product-requirements/ and test-rel-03.0.yaml in docs/specs/test-suites/, based on the project's naming conventions and the fact that main.go references prd005-wc. The use case file rel03.0-uc001-wc should follow the same pattern.\n\nI'm settling on four focused requirements with acceptance criteria that are all verifiable through `go test`, avoiding anything that requires manual judgment. Let me finalize the task structure... Looking at the test suite specification for wc, I need to create differential tests in cmd/wc/wc_test.go that verify the implementation matches GNU wc behavior across default mode, individual flags, and stdin handling scenarios.\n\nThe remaining requirements cover output formatting with right-justified columns matching gwc exactly, and handling the total summary line when processing multiple files. For the test infrastructure, I'm following the established pattern of building the Go binary to a temp directory, locating the GNU reference binary via PATH, and skipping tests gracefully if gwc isn't available.\n\nNow I'm setting up the differential testing framework using testutils.RunDiffTests with DiffTest structs to compare stdout, stderr, and exit codes between the implementations. I'll add a normalizeProgramName function to handle the 'gwc: ' to 'wc: ' conversion in error messages, mirroring the approach used in the cat tests. For test isolation, I'm creating input files with a helper function and using t.TempDir(), while marking all top-level tests with t.Parallel().\n\nThe acceptance criteria confirm the test file needs to compile and pass when gwc is on PATH, with coverage for single-file counting, stdin modes, multi-file totals, and byte-for-byte comparison against gwc across all tests. I'm verifying the design follows established patterns from the existing codebase, particularly how similar test utilities are structured in the cmd/cat and cmd/du implementations.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":68207,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":68207},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"con